    "payload": {
      "message": {
        "role": "assistant",
        "parts": [
          {
            "ord": 0,
            "kind": "text",
            "text": "**Offset-addressed streaming** is a distributed data processing paradigm where events are sequentially appended to an immutable, partitioned commit log and assigned monotonically increasing numerical identifiers known as offsets. Rather than relying on a broker to track message delivery states and consume-and-delete queues, each record's physical position is addressable by its unique offset within a partition. Consumers track their own read progress by persisting their current offset position, effectively decoupling consumers from both producers and one another. This design allows multiple distinct systems to read the identical stream concurrently at independent processing speeds without degrading throughput or mutating the underlying data. Because streams are retained based on configured time or size policies rather than consumption acknowledgments, consumers gain deterministic replayability—enabling them to rewind to historical offsets for fault tolerance, state reconstruction, auditing, or backfilling newly deployed analytical pipelines.",
            "part_id": "msg-571802dc-3d72-5041-b115-b32bdd9538ac-0"
          }
